    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi, i noticed the bottom leaves on my tomato plants are turning a pale yellow color while the top ones look okay. is this a nitrogen issue? also how do i fix the soil ph to help with it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The bottom leaves turning yellow, a classic case of \"gobbling up\" the nutrients, a phenomenon often seen in tomato plants, especially when the soil's pH is off. You're right to suspect a nitrogen issue, but it's not the only culprit. The pale yellow color could be a sign of nutrient deficiencies, overwatering, or even root bound conditions.\n\nTo address the nitrogen issue, you'll want to test your soil's pH and nutrient levels. A soil test will reveal if your soil's pH is too high or too low, which can affect nutrient availability. For tomatoes, a slightly acidic to neutral soil pH (around 6.0-7.0) is ideal. If your soil pH is too high, it's like a \"gobbling up\" situation for the nutrients, making them less available to the plants.\n\nTo adjust the soil pH, you can use lime to raise it or sulfur to lower it. However, it's essential to do this gradually, as sudden changes can shock the plants. For example, if you're raising the pH, add a small amount of lime and mix it well, then wait a few weeks before adding more. This will help the plants adjust to the change, just",
      "source": "target",
