    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what do you mean by \"gobbling up\" the nutrients? that's a weird way to put it. are you saying something is actually eating them or just that they aren't there?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No, no, it's not that the plants are eating the nutrients, although that would be a clever way for them to get a snack. It's more like the plants are \"gobbling up\" the available nutrients, if you will. When the soil's pH is too high or too low, it can create a situation where the nutrients are not readily available to the plants, making them appear to be \"gobbling up\" the nutrients.\n\nThink of it like a group of hungry travelers, all vying for a limited supply of food at a roadside diner. If the diner is too crowded or the food is too scarce, everyone gets a meager meal, even if they're all hungry. Similarly, when the soil's pH is off, the plants are like the hungry travelers, competing for the limited nutrients, and it can lead to nutrient deficiencies.\n\nIn the case of your tomato plants, the bottom leaves turning yellow could be a sign that the plants are not getting enough nutrients, even if they're getting enough overall. It's like they're \"gobbling up\" the available nutrients, leaving the top leaves looking healthy and thriving.",
